  "legal": {
   "import_restricted": [
    "Brazil - the one genuine, ongoing import problem. ANATEL has not granted homologation and buyers reported units seized at customs from 2023; the EFF reported ANATEL had flagged the device as a tool for criminal purposes. This is a certification and import matter, NOT a law against owning one. Flipper's own compliance page lists no sales channel for Brazil, though that is weak corroboration on its own because most countries have no channel listed.",
    "Canada - PROPOSED February 2024, NEVER ENACTED, and demonstrably not in force. ISED announced on 2024-02-08 an intention to ban the Flipper Zero in an auto-theft crackdown; on 2024-03-20 it walked that back to restricting use by illegitimate actors rather than banning the device. As of this check the vendor's own compliance page lists Canada as 'Direct sale'. Many articles and videos still report the ban as though it happened - it did not.",
    "United States - NOT restricted. The device holds FCC Grants of Equipment Authorization for its SRD, BLE and NFC radios. CBP held a shipment of about 15,000 units in late 2022 and later released them. Amazon banned it from its marketplace on 2023-04-07 as a 'card skimming device', but a marketplace policy is not law; the device is legal to own and is sold directly by the vendor.",
    "European Union / United Kingdom - NOT restricted. The vendor holds a CE Declaration of Conformity and a UKCA Declaration of Conformity, and sells direct into Germany, France, the Netherlands, Poland, the UK and others. No enacted member-state ban was found.",
    "Australia - NOT restricted as far as could be confirmed. No ACMA prohibition or import ban was found, and the vendor lists Australia as available via reseller."
   ],
   "risk_level": "medium",
   "notes": "Owning a Flipper Zero is legal in the US, the UK, the EU and most of the world - the device is CE/FCC marked and sold openly by the vendor. What gets people in trouble is USE, not ownership: transmitting on sub-GHz frequencies you are not licensed for, opening a gate or car that is not yours, cloning an access credential without permission, or interfering with a radio service are separate offences almost everywhere, and they are the offences people actually get charged with. Note also that the community firmwares remove a region lock that exists specifically to keep you inside your national band plan - once you flash one, staying legal is on you. Be careful with the reporting on this device: several widely-repeated 'bans' were proposals that were dropped, marketplace policies, or one-off customs and airport-security incidents rather than law. Treat this as 'check your own local law', not legal advice."
  },
