  "stealth": "covert",
  "form_factor": "usb-stick",
  "summary": "A USB-stick-shaped ESP32-S3 with a small colour LCD and a microSD slot hidden inside the USB-A connector housing, running USB Army Knife. Plugged into a target it can be a keyboard, a mass storage device, a CDROM or a USB network adapter, and it can be driven live over its own Wi-Fi from a phone browser - including streaming the victim's screen once an agent is deployed.",
  "honest_take": "This is the most genuinely capable device in this batch for its price, because it attacks over USB rather than over the air - and USB attacks do not care about WPA3 or PMF. Being realistic about the limits: HID injection still needs an unlocked, logged-in machine and the right keyboard layout, and modern EDR increasingly flags a keyboard that types 200 words per minute. The board has no PSRAM (a widely-copied spec claiming 8MB is actually the T-Dongle-S3-Plus, a different model), and LilyGO's own product page carries a note saying 'TF card has no function' which directly contradicts the working SD examples in their GitHub repo - worth testing your specific unit before relying on the card. The embedded Marauder fork gives you deauth and pcap, with all the usual PMF caveats.",
