  "summary": "M5Stack's 48 x 24 mm ESP32-S3 stick running Bruce. It swaps the old StickC Plus2's ESP32-PICO for an ESP32-S3-PICO with 8MB of PSRAM and native USB, adds an IR receiver, and drops Bluetooth Classic. Bruce gives it Wi-Fi, BLE, IR and BadUSB in a keychain-sized package.",
  "honest_take": "The upgrade over the StickC Plus2 is real but two-edged: you gain native USB (so BadUSB now works, which it physically could not before), 8MB PSRAM and an IR receiver, but you lose Bluetooth Classic entirely because the ESP32-S3 has no BR/EDR radio. There is still no microSD slot, so pcap logging and portal credential capture have nowhere to go, and the 250 mAh battery means well under an hour of transmitting. Bruce's sub-GHz, NFC and NRF24 menus are all add-on-module features here too. As a $21 keychain scanner it is good value; as a serious capture tool it is not, and the tiny screen fights Bruce's menu depth. And Bruce's deauth is the same deauth as everyone else's - 802.11w/PMF stops it dead and WPA3 makes PMF mandatory, so treat it as a legacy-network trick rather than a capability.",
