  "honest_take": "The 'Flipper Zero killer' framing in the YouTube coverage is not supportable by the hardware: there is no sub-GHz radio at all - no CC1101, nothing at 433/868/915 MHz - and no 125 kHz LF RFID, which between them are most of what people actually use a Flipper for. The 13.56 MHz NFC reader is an optional extra, not standard. What it is instead is a very well-specified ESP32-S3 handheld: bigger screen, far more memory, audio, motion sensing and a real GPIO header. Judge it as a maker's pocket computer, not as an RF tool. One published figure to distrust: at least one review quotes 2800 mAh; the vendor's own tech-spec page says 1200 mAh.",
  "best_for": [
   "Lua-scriptable ESP32-S3 projects in a finished handheld",
   "Wi-Fi and BLE work with a decent colour screen",
   "People who want a GPIO header and audio in a pocket device"
  ],
